x676c: compose into private storage before publishing panel memory

The default IOSurface is the physical panel allocation. Clearing that allocation
before reading a default-surface layer destroys its input. Compose every x676c
copy frame into one kernel-only buffer, then publish the completed frame before
the existing cache clean and DSI push. This also preserves other panel aliases
and partial/multiple layer inputs without relying on IOSurface object identity.
Keep normal clearing, clipping, strides, layer ordering and bounded source checks.

Allocate exactly one panel frame before surface publication and timer setup;
reject invalid rounded length or allocation failure. Release the new storage on
setup failure and final free. Qualcomm composition remains unchanged.
This corrects deterministic source destruction, not an established whole-UI cause.
Hardware pixel output and physical scanout remain to be validated.

